.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CAT 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm cat
.Nd concatenate and print files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl benstuv
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads each
.Ar file
in turn and writes it to the standard output.
A
.Ar file
of
.Sq Fl ,
or none at all, means the standard input.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Number the lines that are not empty; implies
.Fl n .
.It Fl e
Show a
.Ql $
at the end of each line; implies
.Fl v .
.It Fl n
Number the output lines, starting at 1.
The count continues from one file to the next.
.It Fl s
Squeeze runs of empty lines into a single empty line.
.It Fl t
Show tabs as
.Ql ^I ;
implies
.Fl v .
.It Fl u
Accepted for compatibility:
.Nm
never holds output back past the read that produced it.
.It Fl v
Show non-printing characters visibly: a control character as
.Ql ^
and the character 0100 above it
.Pq Ql ^A ,
DEL as
.Ql ^? ,
and a byte with the high bit set as
.Ql M-
followed by the character for its low seven bits.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Number the lines of two files as one:
.Dl $ cat -n intro.txt body.txt
.Pp
Show where a file has trailing blanks and tabs:
.Dl $ cat -et config
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr more 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq),
whose only option is
.Fl u .
The others are those of the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
